. *****************************************************************************
> ***
. * ACCTG 597A -- Week 8: Confounders, Mediators, Colliders, and the Arrows a D
> esign Assumes Away
. * Gow, I. D., D. F. Larcker, and P. C. Reiss (2016), "Causal Inference in
. * Accounting Research," Journal of Accounting Research 54(2): 477-523.
. *
. * Self-contained simulation, base Stata only (version 17 or later). Run from
. * this folder:  do causal_diagrams.do
. * Figures are written to figures/ and booktabs table fragments to tables/.
. * Runtime about one minute (the Monte Carlo in Module 3 dominates).
. *
. * Modules. In each, the true parameter is known by construction, so the
. * property under study is visible directly. Throughout, the causal effect of
. * x on y is beta = 0.30.
. *   1. One third variable, three roles. The slope of y on x with and without 
> z
. *      as a control when z is a confounder (z -> x, z -> y), a mediator
. *      (x -> z -> y), or a collider (x -> z <- y). Controlling for z removes 
> the
. *      bias in the first case, changes the estimand in the second, and create
> s a
. *      bias in the third. Identity checks: the omitted-variable identity and 
> the
. *      mediation identity hold exactly; the collider slope matches its closed
>  form.
. *   2. Selection on a collider. Keeping only observations with a high value o
> f
. *      c = 0.7 x + 0.7 y + v (a variable caused by both x and y) biases the s
> lope
. *      of y on x in the retained sample, down to a negative slope in the top 
> decile.
. *   3. A small exclusion violation and a weak instrument. The instrument z ha
> s a
. *      direct effect gamma = 0.05 on y (one sixth of beta). The population IV
. *      slope is beta + gamma/pi, so as the first stage pi weakens the IV esti
> mate
. *      moves further from beta than OLS does, although OLS is confounded.
. *****************************************************************************
> ***
. 
